<div class="start-card"><p><strong>You will learn:</strong> image versus container, a Dockerfile's build stages, version tags, and the important Java application boundaries.</p><p><strong>Starting requirements:</strong> stage 02 has created ACR and begun the slow deployments. Docker can run Linux containers. You receive complete working source; do not rewrite the applications.</p><p><strong>Shell and directory:</strong> Bash, repository root. The script selects each correct Docker build context.</p></div>
<h2 id="read">Read the supplied application first</h2>
<p>Open {{file:apps/products/src/main/java/org/tubalabs/products/ProductsController.java}}. The upload endpoint validates the name and UTF-8 <code>.txt</code> file, creates a product with a random product ID and manual ID, then uploads to <code>manuals/products/&lt;productId&gt;/&lt;manualId&gt;.txt</code>. It includes both IDs and a checksum in blob metadata. The IDs are stable for that upload; a different product receives new ones.</p>
<p>Open {{file:apps/products/src/main/java/org/tubalabs/products/Store.java}}. Notice the explicit SQL, <code>JdbcClient</code>, and the small transaction that writes a question and its outbox message together. The scheduled {{file:apps/products/src/main/java/org/tubalabs/products/MessagePump.java}} sends committed outbox entries and consumes replies. These are separate operations because PostgreSQL and Service Bus do not share one atomic transaction.</p>
<p>Thymeleaf templates live under <code>src/main/resources/templates</code>. They render the upload and chat forms on the server. The browser's {{file:apps/products/src/main/resources/static/app.js}} sends requests, polls persistent states and renders text safely using <code>textContent</code>. It does not call Azure models or hold Azure credentials.</p>
<h2 id="dockerfile">Understand the two-stage Dockerfile</h2>
{{code:apps/products/Dockerfile}}
<p>The first <code>FROM</code> supplies Maven and Java 21. <code>WORKDIR</code> selects the container's build directory. Copying <code>pom.xml</code> before the source lets Docker cache dependency downloads. Maven creates <code>target/products.jar</code>. The second stage copies only the JAR into a JRE image and runs as an unprivileged user.</p>
<p>An <strong>image</strong> is the packaged, versioned filesystem and startup configuration. A <strong>container</strong> is a running instance of an image. <code>EXPOSE</code> documents ports; it does not publish them. <code>-p</code> on <code>docker run</code> publishes a port. AKS later runs the same image as a container inside a Pod.</p>
<h2 id="build">Build, tag and push both images</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:build}}
<p>The stage performs <code>docker build</code> for Products and ProductsChat, authenticates Docker to ACR with your Azure sign-in, then performs <code>docker push</code>. The tag looks like <code>w1-…</code> and is derived from the build inputs, not <code>latest</code>. The registry name comes from the shared configuration. You do not replace hostnames throughout the tutorial.</p>
<p>Read {{file:scripts/05-images.sh}}: <code>-t registry/name:tag</code> assigns the full image reference during the build. Giving an existing image another name uses <code>docker tag</code>; it does not rebuild or copy its layers. A tag can move; a digest identifies content. Do not overwrite workshop tags after publishing them.</p>
<p>Products' context is its own directory. The worker context is the repository root so it can include the small shared Python package; the root <code>.dockerignore</code> excludes everything except its required inputs. No <code>.state</code>, secret files, local virtual environments or build credentials enter either image.</p>
<h2 id="run">Run a container without Azure connections</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:inspect-container}}
<p>In another Bash terminal, run <code>curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8080/</code> and <code>curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8080/actuator/health/liveness</code>. The first response explicitly says <strong>Inspect profile</strong>. This profile exposes startup information and health only; upload/chat and Azure connections are disabled. It is a Docker exercise, not a simulated Azure deployment.</p>
<p>Use <code>docker ps</code> to see the running container and <code>docker logs products-inspect</code> to inspect Java startup. Stop it with <code>Ctrl+C</code>; <code>--rm</code> removes this disposable container. The cloud deployment uses the <code>azure</code> profile and requires real dependencies.</p>
<details class="optional"><summary>Optional: practice a separate docker tag command or compile with Wrapper</summary><p>Use the same <code>tag</code> and <code>registry</code> variables from the run block, then run <code>docker tag "$registry/products:$tag" products-local:$tag</code>. Inspect it with <code>docker image inspect "products-local:$tag"</code>. The image ID matches. For a local Java build, change to <code>workshop/apps/products</code> and run <code>bash mvnw test</code> with Java 21. Database integration tests require the disposable PostgreSQL fixture described on the maintainer page.</p></details>
<h2 id="verify">Expected behavior and common fixes</h2>
<div class="verification"><strong>Verification:</strong> both pushes finish with a registry digest, the inspect container responds on localhost, and its health response is <code>{"status":"UP"}</code>. These are expected outputs; see the validation report for checks actually run by the maintainer.</div>
<ul><li><strong>ACR push returns denied:</strong> rerun <code>az acr login</code> through the stage and confirm your subscription has registry push rights. Registry administrator credentials are intentionally disabled.</li><li><strong>Port 8080 is occupied:</strong> use <code>127.0.0.1:8081:8080</code> for this local run and curl port 8081. The AKS manifest still uses its own port 8443.</li><li><strong>Build download fails:</strong> check Docker's internet/proxy access and rerun. Layers cache completed work; do not change library versions just to mask a transient repository failure.</li><li><strong>Exec format error:</strong> rebuild with the supplied <code>--platform linux/amd64</code> flag and confirm Docker supports that platform.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Continue when:</strong> the images are in ACR and the local inspect run works. Now wait for Azure readiness and connect the real services.</p>
